WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.800
Cloud IAM needs monitoring because access changes constantly.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:09.700
Audit logs, sign-in logs, role assignment changes, key creation, policy updates,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:15.120
denied access, trust relationship changes, privilege escalation signals,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:20.300
and unusual API calls all help defenders understand what is happening in the control

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.240
plane.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.500
Logs should be protected, retained according to policy,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.400
and reviewed through security operations and cloud governance workflows.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.260
Sensitive actions deserve particular attention.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:36.760
Creating long-lived access keys, attaching powerful policies, changing role trust,

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.460
granting owner permissions, disabling logging, modifying security tools,

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:45.120
or changing deployment pipelines can materially change risk.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:49.260
Alerts should be tuned so they support investigation rather than generate noise.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:54.200
The best signal explains who acted, what changed, which resource was affected,

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.980
and whether the change matched an approved workflow.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.960
Periodic access review is how teams remove stale privilege.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:06.380
Review human access, service accounts, managed identities, workload identities,

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.720
inactive keys, emergency accounts, exception records, and ownership metadata.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.740
Ask whether the identity still exists for a valid purpose, whether the owner is current,

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.480
whether permissions match the job, whether credentials are active,

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.340
and whether the access should expire.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:28.200
Stale identities are often quiet because no one is actively using them until something

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:29.460
goes wrong.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.120
IAM findings connect to incident response, compliance,

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.420
and cloud security posture management.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.400
A suspicious API call may become an incident lead.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:42.660
A missing access review may become an audit issue.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:46.160
A policy with broad permissions may become a posture finding.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.860
Measurable improvement might mean fewer standing privileges, fewer long-lived keys,

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.520
clearer ownership, faster removal of stale identities,

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.620
and faster detection of risky IAM changes.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.480
Access review should be practical enough to complete.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:06.460
Reviewers need a readable list of identities, owners, roles, last-used data, key age,

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:10.760
privileged assignments, exceptions, and recent sensitive changes.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:16.100
If the review package is a raw export with no context, reviewers may rubber-stamp it.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.400
Good governance turns raw IAM data into decisions people can actually make.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:22.780
Metrics help keep the program honest.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:27.880
Count standing administrator assignments, long-lived keys, inactive identities,

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.100
unowned service accounts, stale emergency accounts, review completion,

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.200
and time to remove access after role changes.

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:38.060
These metrics should not exist only for audit.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.440
They should show whether IAM risk is shrinking and where engineering support is needed.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.820
Governance should include exception handling.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.440
Emergency accounts, vendor access, legacy keys, temporary project grants,

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:53.900
and unusual workload permissions may all be necessary for a time.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:58.920
They should still have owners, reasons, expiration dates, and review schedules.

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:03.540
The difference between a managed exception and unmanaged risk is follow-through.
